I also came across this issue because of an failing test case. I have a model "Company" with an date field 'founded_at':

```php
class Company extends Model
{
    protected $casts = [
        'founded_at' => 'date:Y-m-d',
    ];
}
```

In my tests I use `assertDatabaseHas` and I need to set different `founded_at` values for different versions of Laravel.

When I use it with Laravel version `8.61.0` to make it pass the assertion I need to do:

```php
$this->assertDatabaseHas(
    Company::class,
    ['id' => 10, 'founded_at' => '1991-01-27 00:00:00'],
);
```

While with Laravel version `8.60.0` (and older) it needs to be:

```php
$this->assertDatabaseHas(
    Company::class,
    ['id' => 10, 'founded_at' => '1991-01-27'],
);
```

So in my case this is for a (Laravel) library I am not sure how to solve this issue so it will work for version `8.61.0` and previous versions. I can lock it on `^8.6.1.0`, but in case the mentioned PR is rolled back it would break it again.

There are two app-level solutions for strict SQL date format until/if the framework fixes this:

### Elouqent set mutator

https://laravel.com/docs/8.x/eloquent-mutators#defining-a-mutator

This avoids the new `setAttribute()` behaviour, although you'd need to define a new method for every date column requiring an exact date format.

```php
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Date;

class Company extends Model
{
    protected $casts = [
        'founded_at' => 'date:Y-m-d',
    ];

    public function setFoundedAtAttribute($value)
    {
        $this->attributes['founded_at'] = $value === null ?
            null :
            Date::parse($value)->format('Y-m-d');
    }
}
```

### Custom cast

https://laravel.com/docs/8.x/eloquent-mutators#custom-casts

A re-usable solution that doesn't require a new mutator method for each database column.

```php
namespace App\Casts;

use Illuminate\Contracts\Database\Eloquent\CastsAttributes;
use Illuminate\Contracts\Database\Eloquent\SerializesCastableAttributes;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Date;

class Date implements CastsAttributes, SerializesCastableAttributes
{
    public function get($model, string $key, $value, $attributes)
    {
        if ($value !== null) {
            return Date::parse($value);
        }
    }

    public function set($model, string $key, $value, $attributes)
    {
        if ($value !== null) {
            return Date::parse($value)->format('Y-m-d');
        }
    }

    public function serialize($model, string $key, $value, array $attributes)
    {
        return $this->set($model, $key, $value, $attributes);
    }
}
```

```php
class Company extends Model
{
    protected $casts = [
        'founded_at' => \App\Casts\Date::class,
    ];
}
```

---

For both snippets, `Date::parse()` may not be the catch-all solution since protected method [`Model@asDateTime()`](https://github.com/laravel/framework/blob/6216534f14c7cb8bdfa213e5f7e3d357119a4579/src/Illuminate/Database/Eloquent/Concerns/HasAttributes.php#L1048) used by `setAttribute()` has some additional ways it constructs a new Carbon object.

I’m so sorry for the problems as a result of #38720.

@EgorGruzdev / @derekmd / @Ilyes512 if you have a chance, would you mind testing #38994 to see if it fixes the issues you had? As far as I can tell, it restores the formatting for the database bindings while retaining the comparison check I was attempting to fix.

@taylorotwell would you reconsider your possible policy change for that followup PR?

